## Migration: `Thread.Abort` Timeout → Cooperative Cancellation (.NET 8)

### Pattern Identified: **Timeout Enforcement**

Your code forcibly aborts a thread after a timeout. In .NET 6+, `Thread.Abort()` throws `PlatformNotSupportedException` and `Thread.ResetAbort()` is gone (`SYSLIB0006`).

### Migrated Code

```csharp
public class TimedExecutor
{
    public T ExecuteWithTimeout<T>(Func<T> work, TimeSpan timeout)
    {
        using var cts = new CancellationTokenSource(timeout);
        var task = Task.Run(work, cts.Token);

        try
        {
            // Task.WaitAsync throws OperationCanceledException on timeout
            return task.WaitAsync(cts.Token).GetAwaiter().GetResult();
        }
        catch (OperationCanceledException) when (cts.IsCancellationRequested)
        {
            throw new TimeoutException(
                $"Operation exceeded {timeout.TotalSeconds}s timeout");
        }
        catch (AggregateException ex) when (ex.InnerExceptions.Count == 1)
        {
            throw new AggregateException(ex.InnerException!);
        }
    }

    // Preferred: async version
    public async Task<T> ExecuteWithTimeoutAsync<T>(
        Func<CancellationToken, Task<T>> work, TimeSpan timeout)
    {
        using var cts = new CancellationTokenSource(timeout);

        try
        {
            return await work(cts.Token);
        }
        catch (OperationCanceledException) when (cts.IsCancellationRequested)
        {
            throw new TimeoutException(
                $"Operation exceeded {timeout.TotalSeconds}s timeout");
        }
    }
}
```

### Key Pitfalls

| Pitfall | Mitigation |
|---------|------------|
| **Work ignores cancellation** | If `work` is a tight CPU loop that never checks a token, cancellation won't stop it — the task keeps running in the background. Refactor `work` to accept `CancellationToken` and call `token.ThrowIfCancellationRequested()` at checkpoints. |
| **Leaking `CancellationTokenSource`** | Always wrap in `using`. It holds timers internally. |
| **Fire-and-forget task** | The sync version returns on timeout but the task may still be running. If `work` holds resources, pass the token so it can exit cleanly. |
| **`Thread.ResetAbort` logic** | Replaced entirely — cooperative cancellation means you check `IsCancellationRequested` and *decide* whether to continue. |
| **Uncooperative third-party code** | If you truly cannot modify `work` to check the token, move it to a **child process** and use `Process.Kill()`. There is no in-process equivalent to `Thread.Abort` in modern .NET. |

### If Your Work Can Cooperate (Best Practice)

```csharp
// Caller
var result = await executor.ExecuteWithTimeoutAsync(
    async ct =>
    {
        foreach (var item in items)
        {
            ct.ThrowIfCancellationRequested(); // checkpoint
            await ProcessAsync(item, ct);
        }
        return ComputeResult();
    },
    TimeSpan.FromSeconds(30));
```

### Summary of API Replacements

| .NET Framework | .NET 8 |
|----------------|--------|
| `thread.Abort()` | `cts.Cancel()` / `CancelAfter(timeout)` |
| `catch (ThreadAbortException)` | `catch (OperationCanceledException)` |
| `Thread.ResetAbort()` | Check `token.IsCancellationRequested`, continue if desired |
| `thread.Join(timeout)` + abort | `Task.WaitAsync(token)` or `Task.WhenAny` with delay |

The fundamental shift: cancellation is now **cooperative** — the running code must voluntarily check the token and exit. Design checkpoint frequency based on your acceptable cancellation latency.
